# Shared secret for the web UI. Required: with this blank every route is open,
# including the one that starts billed agents with write access to your
# workspaces, and the app refuses to start rather than serve that silently.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
UF_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The only way to run with no authentication, and it has to be typed. Set to 1
# ONLY if the port is bound to loopback and you are the only user of the
# machine; every page then carries a banner saying the app is unauthenticated.
# Any other value — including "true" — is read as "no".
UF_ALLOW_NO_AUTH=

# Optional, read-only, and for a monitoring system rather than a person. Set it
# and `/api/status` accepts it as `Authorization: Bearer …` and answers with
# gauges — queue depth, window spend, store sizes, sweeper liveness. It reaches
# that one route and nothing else, which is the whole point: UF_AUTH_TOKEN above
# is the credential that can also start billed agents, so a scraper should never
# hold it.
#
# Left blank, /api/status is not exempt from the gate at all: a monitor gets a
# 401 rather than the endpoint being public.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
UF_STATUS_TOKEN=

# Optional, and the other half of running unattended: where this container POSTs
# when a run reaches an ending that needs a person. Five of them — needs-review,
# a guard that stopped the run, a crash, a run refused before its first cycle,
# and the first rung of the 429 ladder. Blank is off, which is the shipped state.
#
# One generic signed JSON body — install, event, run_id, status, at, url — goes
# to one URL you name. Nothing here knows about any vendor, and that is
# deliberate: no task text, no folder, no branch, no cost, and never the run's
# title, which is text a model can write.
#
# Discord and Slack cannot be pointed at directly. Their incoming webhooks parse
# only their own body shape ({"content": …} and {"text": …}), so a bare Discord
# URL answers 400 "Cannot send an empty message", code 50006 — measured against
# a live webhook on 2026-08-24. Home Assistant's POST /api/webhook/<webhook_id>
# accepts arbitrary JSON and is the shaping layer that fans out to either. ntfy
# and a Telegram bot need the same kind of relay. For Discord on its own,
# scripts/discord-relay.mjs is that layer in one file: point this variable at
# the relay rather than at Discord, and set the DISCORD_* keys below. See
# docs/install.md, "Being told".
#   UF_WEBHOOK_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123/api/webhook/uf-run-ended
#   UF_WEBHOOK_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8787/uf
UF_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Required whenever the URL above is set: the HMAC key for
# `X-UF-Signature: sha256=<hex>`, computed over the exact bytes of that body.
# Nothing is delivered while this is blank, and the container log says so per
# lost notification. A Home Assistant webhook id is an unauthenticated URL by
# design, so this signature is the only thing that tells your automation this
# install's POST from anything else that can reach the instance.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
UF_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Optional. How this install is reached from wherever you read the notification.
# It is the only absolute URL this app ever builds — every other one is relative
# — and it exists for the `url` field of that body, which is the link that takes
# you to the run. Blank sends an empty one.
#   UF_PUBLIC_URL=https://uf.example.com
UF_PUBLIC_URL=

# Optional. A name for this install, for an operator who runs more than one. It
# is the `install` field of that body and nothing else reads it. Not a hostname
# unless you want one on the wire — anything you would rather not send to your
# receiver should not go here.
#   UF_INSTALL_LABEL=laptop
UF_INSTALL_LABEL=

# Optional. Set to 1 to also be told when a run simply finished. Blank is off
# and is the shipped state: the four endings above are the ones that want a
# person, and at a fleet's scale a notification per success is how a channel
# stops being read — which costs you the endings it exists for. Worth turning on
# if you run a handful of runs a day and want the "it is done" signal. It widens
# `completed` and nothing else; the statuses a run passes through stay silent,
# and so does a run you cancelled yourself.
#   UF_NOTIFY_ON_SUCCESS=1
UF_NOTIFY_ON_SUCCESS=

# The keys below configure scripts/discord-relay.mjs, which runs *inside* the
# container and is started by docker-entrypoint.sh when DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL is
# set. Setting that one key is the whole switch; nothing needs starting by hand.
#
# UF_WEBHOOK_URL above must then name the relay, because blank still means
# notifications are off and the entrypoint will not quietly change that:
#   UF_WEBHOOK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/uf
# The boot log says so if you set one and not the other.
#
# The relay listens on loopback inside the container, so it is reachable from
# nowhere else — not from your LAN, not from the host, whatever the firewall
# says. The port is not published and does not need to be.

# The Discord channel webhook to forward to. Server Settings → Integrations →
# Webhooks → New Webhook → Copy Webhook URL. Note that a webhook posts to its
# channel and cannot send a DM; that would need a bot token and a guild you
# share with it.
#
# It is a credential — anyone holding it can post to that channel — and it is
# handled as one. The entrypoint hands it to the relay and then **unsets it**
# before starting the server, because orchestrator.ts builds every agent's
# environment as { ...process.env }: a variable the server still held would be
# readable by every unattended agent. Do not add it anywhere else in
# docker-compose.yml without answering that.
#   DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/<id>/<token>
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional, and it is the half that makes a notification reach a phone. A
# message in a channel is read when somebody looks at the channel, which is the
# same silence UF_WEBHOOK_URL exists to end; a mention is what Discord turns
# into a push. It is also the only id the relay permits Discord to act on —
# allowed_mentions is sent as a whitelist, so an install label reading @everyone
# stays characters. Settings → Advanced → Developer Mode, then right-click
# yourself → Copy User ID.
#   DISCORD_MENTION_USER_ID=123456789012345678
DISCORD_MENTION_USER_ID=

# Optional. Forces the session cookie's Secure flag on (1) or off (0). Leave
# blank and it follows the request: X-Forwarded-Proto first, then the URL. Set
# it to 1 only if your TLS terminator does not send that header — a browser
# never returns a Secure cookie over plain HTTP, so getting this wrong looks
# like a sign-in that works once and then does not.
UF_COOKIE_SECURE=

# Which host interface compose publishes the port on. Blank means 127.0.0.1 —
# reachable from this machine only, which is what makes UF_ALLOW_NO_AUTH above
# defensible at all.
#
# Set it to 0.0.0.0 (or one interface's address) to reach the app from other
# machines on your network, and change two things with it: UF_AUTH_TOKEN must be
# set and UF_ALLOW_NO_AUTH blank, because the routes now exposed start billed
# agents with write access to the workspaces below; and UF_COOKIE_SECURE must be
# 0 rather than 1 while there is no TLS in front. Neither is checked here —
# compose publishes what it is told.
#
# It is still a shared secret over plain HTTP: anyone able to watch the network
# sees the token. That is a different bar from the internet, not a substitute
# for TLS. Do not point this at an interface a router forwards to.
# UF_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# The host port to publish on. Only interesting for a second instance alongside
# the first — see the note at the top of docker-compose.yml.
# UF_PORT=3000

# Optional. Enables the "API account" panel (rate limits + billed cost).
# Must be an organization Admin key — regular sk-ant-api… keys will 401, and
# individual (non-organization) accounts cannot create one at all.
ANTHROPIC_ADMIN_KEY=

# Optional. Lets a run use GitHub: `git push`, `gh pr create`, `gh issue view`,
# and cloning or fetching a private repository. The container has no other
# credential for it — the mounted ~/.claude carries your Claude login only, not
# ~/.gitconfig, ~/.ssh or ~/.config/gh — so without this those commands fail
# part-way through a run rather than at the start of one.
#
# A fine-grained token limited to the repositories you run agents against, with
# Contents: read and write (plus Pull requests / Issues if the agent should open
# them). A classic token needs `repo`. An unattended agent can use everything
# this token can, so do not hand it one that reaches your whole account.
#
# It also makes an SSH remote work: the container holds no key, so a
# git@github.com: remote is rewritten to HTTPS for agents only, never for the
# git this app itself runs.
UF_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Optional, and the way to follow the advice above once you point this at more
# than one or two repositories. One token for every repository means an agent
# fixing a test in one of them holds a credential that can force-push to all the
# others; there is no way to narrow that from inside a run, because the
# credential helper answers for github.com as a whole.
#
# `folder=token` entries separated by |, where the folder is written as the
# picker shows it (relative to a workspace) or absolute (/workspace/acme/web). A
# run working in that folder — or anywhere under it — gets that token and no
# other. A folder no entry names falls back to UF_GITHUB_TOKEN above, so leave
# that blank to give every unnamed repository no credential at all.
#
# An entry with an empty token means "this repository gets none", which is how
# you exclude one while keeping an install-wide token for the rest.
#
#   UF_GITHUB_TOKENS=acme/web=github_pat_aaa|acme/api=github_pat_bbb|acme/secret=
#
# The orchestrator chat still uses UF_GITHUB_TOKEN: it looks across every
# workspace before it knows which repository it is proposing work in.
UF_GITHUB_TOKENS=

# Optional. `gh` extensions to have in the container, installed at boot and kept
# in a named volume so they survive the next `docker compose up --build`.
# Installing one by hand in a shell does not: gh keeps extensions in the
# container's writable layer, which that command discards, and what an agent
# meets afterwards is `unknown command` in the middle of a work cycle.
#
# One `owner/repo` per extension, separated by spaces (commas and | also work).
# Add `@tag` to pin a version, which is worth doing for the same reason a
# dependency is pinned — an extension is an executable your agents run, with the
# run's GitHub token in its environment:
#
#   UF_GH_EXTENSIONS=dlvhdr/gh-dash github/gh-copilot@v1.1.0
#
# Needs UF_GITHUB_TOKEN above, even for a public extension: gh refuses every API
# call without a credential. A private repository additionally needs that token
# to have read access to it.
#
# The list is consulted once per extension. An entry already installed is
# skipped — including one whose @tag has since changed, because a restart is not
# a good moment to silently swap out an executable that holds a token. To move a
# pin or drop an extension, edit the line and then remove the old one yourself:
#
#   docker compose exec usagefoundry sh -c 'GH_TOKEN=$UF_GITHUB_TOKEN gh extension list'
#   docker compose exec usagefoundry sh -c 'GH_TOKEN=$UF_GITHUB_TOKEN gh extension remove dash'
#
# and restart. `docker compose down -v` discards them all with the volume, and
# the next boot reinstalls whatever this line still names.
UF_GH_EXTENSIONS=

# Optional. Python tools to have in the container, installed at boot and kept in
# a named volume so they survive the next `docker compose up --build`.
#
# This is how a Claude Code plugin whose hooks shell out to a Python command
# works here. The image ships python3 for node-gyp and nothing to install a
# package with — no pip, no ensurepip, and a Debian marker refusing a
# system-wide install — so the command a hook runs is simply absent. That does
# not surface as a broken plugin: hook bodies end in `|| true`, so the hook
# exits 0 having done nothing, and a plugin that announces itself on session
# start goes on announcing itself. Measured on one install here: 213 sessions
# told a plugin was active against a command that was never present.
#
# One PEP 508 requirement per tool, separated by spaces or "|" — not commas,
# which are meaningful inside a version specifier. Pin the version for the same
# reason you would pin a dependency; this is an executable your agents run:
#
#   UF_PY_TOOLS=cozempic==1.8.39
#
# Needs no token. Tools come from PyPI, so the container needs outbound network
# at boot and nothing else.
#
# Your own copy of one, rather than the published release, is a *container*
# path — a directory under one of the workspace mounts below, not a host path:
#
#   UF_PY_TOOLS=/workspace/winnow
#
# That is installed editable and re-run on every boot. Editable means the
# container follows the source rather than copying it, so an edit you make on
# the host is live in the next session with nothing to reinstall; the boot-time
# re-run is what picks up a change to the parts editable does not follow, its
# dependencies and its console scripts. A tree too broken to build leaves the
# last good install in place and says so in the log.
#
# For a copy pinned to the state it was installed in, name it and give the path
# as a URL instead — that one is copied once and skipped on later boots:
#
#   UF_PY_TOOLS=cozempic@file:///workspace/winnow
#
# Worth knowing before choosing the first: the agents can write the mounts, and
# an editable install means code under one of them runs on every session start,
# as a hook, with no restart in between. That is the same reach a plugin
# directory already has — its hooks come from a mount too — but it is a second
# door onto it, and the pinned form above is the one that does not have it.
#
# Two things to turn off in the tool itself, if it has them. Neither belongs in
# this file: compose reads it for interpolation only, and there is no env_file,
# so a name docker-compose.yml does not forward never reaches the container.
# They go in a docker-compose.override.yml, which is where one host's answer to
# a third-party tool belongs anyway:
#
#   services:
#     usagefoundry:
#       environment:
#         COZEMPIC_NO_GLOBAL_INIT: "1"
#         COZEMPIC_NO_AUTO_UPDATE: "1"
#
# From there they reach the agents and their hooks, because childEnv strips only
# UF_*, OTEL_* and four named keys.
#
#   An auto-updater. The pin above is only the version that gets *installed*,
#   and a tool that upgrades itself on each session start is one whose version
#   nobody chose. For cozempic: COZEMPIC_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1.
#
#   Anything that installs itself into ~/.claude. That directory is a bind
#   mount of your own — the same file your host's Claude Code reads — so a tool
#   that "wires itself in globally" on first run edits your machine's settings
#   from inside the container, for every session on it and not just this app's.
#   Measured, not guessed: one `cozempic --version` in a throwaway container
#   wrote 7 hooks into ~/.claude/settings.json. For cozempic:
#   COZEMPIC_NO_GLOBAL_INIT=1. Its own plugin hooks already set it; what they
#   do not cover is an agent, or you, running the command directly.
#
# The list is consulted once per tool. An entry already installed is skipped,
# including one whose version has since changed — a restart is not a good moment
# to silently swap out an executable a hook runs. To move a pin or drop a tool,
# edit the line and then remove the old one yourself:
#
#   docker compose exec usagefoundry uv tool list
#   docker compose exec usagefoundry uv tool uninstall cozempic
#
# and restart. `docker compose down -v` discards them all with the volume, and
# the next boot reinstalls whatever this line still names.
UF_PY_TOOLS=

# ─── winnow's intake filter ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One switch. "1" starts a loopback proxy inside the container at boot and
# points every agent at it with ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL; blank is off and is the
# shipped state.
#
# What it does: a tool result a rule marks as spent — a Glob, an `ls`, a passing
# `npm test` — is sent in full on the one request where the agent acts on it,
# placed after the last cache_control breakpoint so the API never writes it to
# the prompt cache, and dropped from the next request. Those bytes cost 1.0x
# once instead of a 2.0x cache write plus a 0.1x read on every later turn.
#
# What it is worth, measured over 175 sessions of this operator's own corpus:
# it reaches 8.21% of message content and nets +3.76% of the bill, and unlike
# an after-the-fact prune it cannot come out negative, because nothing already
# cached is ever edited. What is *not* measured is whether an agent that loses
# a `git status` on the turn after it read it does worse work. It re-runs the
# command; nobody has checked what that costs in turns.
#
# Needs a winnow checkout the container can see — a path under one of the
# workspace mounts, not a host path. It is not installed and not on PATH: the
# entrypoint runs it out of the checkout with uv, in a virtualenv of its own
# under /home/node, so it never touches the .venv you work in on the host.
#
# Fails soft in both directions. No checkout, or nothing listening within 90
# seconds, and the boot says so and leaves ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL unset — every
# agent then talks to the API directly, exactly as with this off. Do not set
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL yourself: pointed at a proxy that never started, every
# agent request fails with a connection refused inside a tool call.
#
#   WINNOW_FILTER=1
#
# Turning it off again, and the two are not equivalent:
#
#   Fully off       set this blank and `docker compose up -d`. No proxy, no
#                   ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, boot identical to before.
#   Off, no restart docker compose exec usagefoundry \
#                     touch /var/lib/winnow/filter-off
#                   The proxy keeps listening and keeps relaying; it stops
#                   rewriting, from the next request. `rm` the file to resume.
#
# Only the second is safe on a running install. ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL is fixed in
# an agent's environment when it is spawned, so killing the proxy does not turn
# the filter off — it turns the API off for every agent until the next restart.
#
WINNOW_FILTER=

# Where the checkout is, as the *container* sees it. Only read when the switch
# above is on.
WINNOW_FILTER_PATH=

# Loopback port for the filter. Not 8787 — the Discord relay binds that inside
# this same container, and the second process to want a port gets nothing.
WINNOW_FILTER_PORT=8789

# Which winnow the image bundles, for context pruning.
#
# BUILD ARGUMENTS, not environment: compose passes them to `docker build` and
# nothing inside the container reads them. Both default in the Dockerfile, so
# leaving these blank-and-commented builds exactly what everyone else builds.
#
# Context pruning is the only thing bounding a work cycle's context — it
# replaced Claude Code's `--autocompact`, which this app no longer sends — so
# the clone is a hard failure at build time rather than a warning at boot. An
# install that could not fetch it is one whose long cycles have no ceiling.
#
# Set WINNOW_REF to an empty value (not commented out — actually `WINNOW_REF=`)
# to build without it, on a host that cannot reach GitHub. The feature then
# reports itself unavailable in the run log rather than pruning nothing quietly.
#
# Pinned to a commit rather than a branch: this app measures what the tool
# removes and prices it, so an unpinned rebuild would move that contract with
# nothing announcing it.
#
#   WINNOW_REPO=https://github.com/Xapicc/winnow.git
#   WINNOW_REF=0421da53d5087c1746c3101d0c97d0682d3094ac

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workspaces — the host directories the agent may work in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Four, and four is a real ceiling. Each appears as a "Workspace" choice in the
# New run form, and the folder a run is *given* is containment-checked against
# the one workspace it was started in — a path valid in one is rejected in
# another.
#
# That is a check on the run's folder, not a sandbox around the agent. A Bash
# call is not bounded by the working directory, and every mount below is named
# on the child's --add-dir, so treat all four as one blast radius. The same
# agent can read the mounted ~/.claude, which holds your Claude account's own
# credential — see docs/security.md, "What an agent can still reach".
#
# A slot is switched on by giving it a NAME. Slots 2-4 without a NAME are
# ignored no matter what path they name, so uncomment both lines for each extra
# directory you want.
#
# A fifth directory needs a volume line, which cannot come from this file —
# compose cannot add a mount conditionally. Setting UF_WORKSPACE_5_NAME here
# makes the container refuse to start and say so, rather than ignoring it: a
# configured slot that was never mounted looks exactly like a mounted slot with
# nothing in it. Either put the extra repositories under a directory that is
# already mounted, or add the mount in a docker-compose.override.yml — see
# docs/install.md, "More than four workspaces".

# Slot 1 is required. Left unset on purpose: `docker compose up` refuses to
# start without it, which is louder than mounting a plausible-looking path that
# does not exist. Absolute path, no `~`.
UF_WORKSPACE_NAME=Workspace
# Where your Claude Code directory lives, when the shell does not say.
#
# Leave blank on macOS and Linux: compose reads HOME from the invoking shell
# and that is the whole of it. Set it on Windows, where PowerShell and `cmd`
# set USERPROFILE rather than HOME and compose would otherwise refuse to
# start — for example:
#
#   UF_CLAUDE_DIR=C:/Users/you
#
# Forward slashes; Docker Desktop accepts them and they avoid a backslash
# being read as an escape. Git Bash and WSL set HOME, so they need nothing
# here. The directory itself must exist: Docker creates a missing bind source
# rather than refusing, and an empty one reads as a dashboard of zeros.
UF_CLAUDE_DIR=

UF_WORKSPACE=

# UF_WORKSPACE_2_NAME=Notes
# UF_WORKSPACE_2=/home/you/Documents/Notes

# UF_WORKSPACE_3_NAME=Scratch
# UF_WORKSPACE_3=/home/you/scratch

# UF_WORKSPACE_4_NAME=
# UF_WORKSPACE_4=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux only — the uid the agents run as.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every child this app spawns — the work cycle, the reviewer, the chat, and the
# git it runs itself — writes to the mounted ~/.claude and to your workspaces,
# so it has to own them. macOS Docker Desktop remaps bind-mount ownership and
# the default 1000 is always right there; Linux does not, and a mismatch shows
# up as git refusing every repository and `/login` never sticking. On Linux:
#
#   UF_UID=$(id -u)
#   UF_GID=$(id -g)
#
# The *server* is not this uid: it runs as root inside the container and drops
# each child to the pair above, which is what makes /proc, the database and the
# MCP capability file unreachable from an agent. docs/security.md has the whole
# argument, including what it does not buy.
#
# The SQLite volume needs nothing from you either way: it is a named volume, so
# its permissions come from the image — root-owned 0700 — and the entrypoint
# reclaims one created by an earlier release, which used to be world-writable
# and therefore rewritable by any agent.

# UF_UID=1000
# UF_GID=1000

# The orchestrator chat and a workflow's orchestrator block run as the same uid
# with a *different group*, and that group is what owns the capability file each
# turn hands its child. Without it, one of twenty-five concurrent agents can read
# that file off a sibling's command line and call /api/mcp as the chat.
#
# Leave this alone unless 65533 is a real group on your host that your agents are
# in. It must differ from UF_GID: the server refuses to boot when they match,
# rather than handing the file to the group it is being kept from. Clearing it
# keeps the uid split and drops this one boundary; the boot log says which.

# UF_CHAT_GID=65533

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sandbox around what an agent's commands may touch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off, and off is the shipped state: with UF_SANDBOX blank nothing is written,
# nothing is confined, and this install behaves exactly as it did before these
# three variables existed. The uid above says *who* an agent is; this says what
# it may reach, and today the honest answer is "everything that uid can".
#
# Set UF_SANDBOX=1 and the container writes a policy to
# /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json at boot — root-owned, which is the
# point: the agents are UF_UID and cannot edit it, and a repository's own
# .claude/settings.json is ignored for these keys. The policy asks Claude Code
# for four things, each of them what its own settings schema says it does:
#
#   - a read deny over /data and /backups, so the database every guard reads,
#     the settings behind it and any snapshot of them are out of reach of a
#     run's shell even on an install that runs everything as one uid;
#   - a credential deny over ~/.claude/.credentials.json, so `cat` of your
#     Claude token fails inside a run while the session still authenticates
#     and bills — the one thing on this list nothing else here can do, and the
#     one most worth confirming before you rely on it;
#   - dangerouslyDisableSandbox switched off, so a model cannot ask for a
#     command to run outside the sandbox;
#   - egress limited to the domains you name below, if you name any.
#
# **Read this before setting it.** It needs a widened seccomp profile, which
# this container does not get by default: the `security_opt` block at the bottom
# of docker-compose.yml, put in a docker-compose.override.yml rather than
# uncommented there — compose merges an override automatically, and which
# profile a daemon accepts is one machine's answer rather than this project's.
# Without it bubblewrap cannot create a namespace at all. docs/install.md,
# "Optional: turn on the sandbox", is the whole procedure.
#
# Turn this on without that and you are in one of **three** states. Two are the
# ones you would expect: every `claude` exits non-zero fleet-wide
# (UF_SANDBOX_ENFORCEMENT below at its default), or every command runs
# unconfined behind a warning nobody reads ("warn"). Both are recoverable by
# editing this file and restarting — neither needs a rebuild, which is why the
# policy is generated at boot rather than baked into the image.
#
# The third is the one this install landed in, and it is the reason to read this
# far. The CLI's availability check asks only whether the `bwrap` and `socat`
# binaries exist and are executable. It never runs one. So an installed
# bubblewrap that the kernel then refuses passes that check, "refuse" never
# fires, nothing stops — and every `Bash` call is instead wrapped in a program
# that exits 1 before the command runs. That state lasted fifteen hours here:
# ten runs, 214 failed `Bash` calls, $407 of spend, and from outside
# every one of them looked like an agent that had stopped being productive. A
# `sandbox` row on the run's log is what tells those two apart.
#
# **And the profile is necessary without being sufficient.** With it applied,
# bubblewrap builds its namespace here (measured, at both uids, on Docker Engine
# 29.7.2 / kernel 6.12.76-linuxkit). What still fails afterwards is a fresh
# procfs: Docker masks parts of /proc, so the CLI's *default* bubblewrap shape
# dies with "Can't mount proc on /newroot/proc: Operation not permitted", as
# root as well. docker-entrypoint.sh therefore writes
# `"enableWeakerNestedSandbox": true` into the managed policy on every boot,
# unconditionally, which switches the CLI to binding the existing /proc — the
# one shape that starts here. Nothing for you to set; it is named because it is
# the reason the seccomp profile alone leaves you in the third state above. The
# CLI's name for it is honest: a sandboxed command sees this container's /proc,
# so a sibling agent's processes are visible in it.
#
# What it is *not*. It does not confine a run to its own checkout: the policy
# is install-wide and names no per-run paths, so a run can still write a
# concurrent run's worktree and the branch that worktree will land. That is a
# later piece of work — see docs/security.md, "What an agent can still reach".
#
# And the part no document can soften. What has now been executed here is the
# half above — bubblewrap starting, and bubblewrap failing to — plus two
# `claude -p` calls against a working one: a shell command that ran, and a
# `.credentials.json` that came back "Permission denied" to the uid that owns
# it, which is the credential deny enforced rather than written down. **No work
# cycle has run under a sandbox that started.** The egress allowlist has never
# been exercised; no per-run write set has ever reached a live sandbox; whether
# the set each child is given is wide enough for a real work cycle is reasoning
# rather than a measurement. Treat
# switching it on as an experiment you are running: verify it yourself
# (docs/verification.md names every step), watch the first run rather than the
# second, and do not assume a policy that is in place is a policy that is
# working — the boot line and Settings → Sandbox both report the *file*.
# UF_SANDBOX=1

# What happens when the CLI *knows* the sandbox is unavailable — bwrap or socat
# missing from the image, the seccomp applier gone. "refuse" (the default, and
# what blank means) exits the CLI with an error, so the failure arrives on the
# run's own log with a reason on it. "warn" runs the command anyway,
# unconfined.
#
# Refusing is the loud option and the one to start with: a fleet that stops is
# a fleet you fix, where a fleet that quietly confines nothing looks exactly
# like one that works. Lower it to "warn" deliberately, not to make an error
# go away.
#
# What neither value reaches is the third state above. The check behind this
# switch tests that two binaries exist, never that they work, so a bubblewrap
# the kernel refuses is not "unavailable" to it — "refuse" stops nothing and
# "warn" warns about nothing, and the failure surfaces one layer down as a
# `Bash` call that did not run. That is what the `sandbox` row on a run's log
# is for.
# UF_SANDBOX_ENFORCEMENT=refuse

# Domains sandboxed commands may reach, separated by "|" (commas and spaces
# also work). Blank leaves egress alone, which is deliberate: your agents run
# `npm install`, `go mod download`, `gh` and whatever the repository needs, and
# a list this project guessed at would fail inside a tool call the run loop
# does not read. There is no safe default; there is only your list.
#
# Naming even one domain does two things at once. It limits egress to that list
# — enforced by a proxy the sandboxed command has no route around, provided the
# seccomp applier in the image is working — and it pins the list to the managed
# file, so a run cannot widen it by appending to the ~/.claude/settings.json it
# can write. Start from what a cycle actually needs and expect to add to it:
#
#   UF_SANDBOX_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=api.anthropic.com|github.com|registry.npmjs.org
# UF_SANDBOX_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who owns the mounted ~/.claude.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off, and off is the shipped state: with this blank, ~/.claude keeps the
# ownership it has now — yours on the host, the agents' inside the container —
# and this install behaves exactly as it did before this variable existed.
#
# What it is for. `~/.claude/settings.json` is one of the files Claude Code
# reads a sandbox policy out of, and it is owned by the same uid your agents run
# as. A run can therefore append
#
#   {"sandbox":{"filesystem":{"allowWrite":["/"]}}}
#
# to it, and the next session — its own, or a sibling's — starts confined to
# nothing. Until this is on, UF_SANDBOX above and the per-run limits this app
# passes each `claude` are both narrower than what a run can grant itself, and
# the same file also carries hooks, permission rules and environment for every
# session the CLI starts. Set UF_LOCK_CLAUDE_HOME=1 and the container gives
# ~/.claude and that settings.json to root at boot, then hands back, one by one,
# the entries the CLI writes — projects/ (which is every usage figure and every
# budget guard), sessions/, todos/, shell-snapshots/, history.jsonl,
# .credentials.json, .claude.json and backups/. It is all or nothing: if any of
# those cannot be handed back, nothing is changed and the boot log says which
# one and why.
#
# **The part that reaches outside the container.** ~/.claude is a bind mount of
# your own home directory, and this changes it on the host too. After it, on
# Linux:
#
#   - your own Claude Code still runs, still writes transcripts, sessions and
#     history, because those entries are still yours;
#   - you can no longer create anything new at the top level of ~/.claude, and
#     you can no longer edit ~/.claude/settings.json — including through
#     `/config` — without `sudo`;
#   - clearing this variable and restarting the container puts it back, and by
#     hand it is two paths and no `-R`, because nothing below them was taken:
#     `sudo chown "$(id -u):$(id -g)" ~/.claude ~/.claude/settings.json`.
#
# On macOS, Docker Desktop emulates bind-mount ownership, so the chown may not
# reach your host files at all — and may not confine the agents either. The
# entrypoint checks from the agents' own uid and says so on the boot log if the
# change did not take. Read that line before believing this is on.
#
# "1" is the only value that switches this on — any other non-empty value is
# off, with a boot log line saying the directory was left writable. Confirm the
# container was actually given it before you conclude anything from that log:
#
#     docker compose exec usagefoundry sh -c 'echo "[$UF_LOCK_CLAUDE_HOME]"'
#
# Turn it on in this order, or it will refuse: sign in — from Settings → Claude
# account, or from a shell as the uid the agents run as, which is not what an
# exec defaults to and not what a ${UF_UID} typed at your own shell expands to,
# because .env never reaches that shell:
#
#     uid=$(docker compose exec -T usagefoundry printenv UF_AGENT_UID)
#     docker compose exec -u "$uid" -it usagefoundry claude
#
# then /login — and let one real work cycle finish first, so the CLI has
# created the entries it needs. A locked ~/.claude is one it cannot create
# anything in, and a missing projects/ would be a dashboard of zeros rather
# than an error. The boot refuses outright if projects/ is not there yet, and
# warns rather than refuses for the rest. Nothing here is verified: see
# docs/verification.md, and check it on your own install before relying on it.
# UF_LOCK_CLAUDE_HOME=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory — how much transcript history stays parsed in RAM.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard reads every .jsonl under ~/.claude and keeps what it parsed, so
# a scan only has to read the bytes appended since the last one. That retention
# is bounded: past this many turns the coldest files are dropped and re-read
# from disk the next time a scan needs them. Nothing about the numbers on the
# page changes when that happens — only how long a refresh takes.
#
# Roughly 330 bytes per turn, so the default of 500000 is ~165 MB against the
# ~2 GB heap Node gives itself on an 8 GB host. Raise it if you have the memory
# and a lot of history; the dashboard's header strip shows the heap in use, and
# says when the cache is at its bound.

# UF_TRANSCRIPT_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES=500000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where backups are written.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `scripts/backup-db.mjs` writes a consistent snapshot of the database into
# /backups inside the container, which is this directory on the host. It is a
# bind mount rather than the named volume the database itself lives in,
# precisely so a snapshot survives `docker compose down -v`.
#
# The default is ./backups in this checkout, which the repository ships empty so
# it works with no setup. If you point this somewhere else, create the directory
# first: Docker creates a missing bind source owned by root, and the container
# runs as UF_UID above.
#
# Nothing is written until you run the script — see docs/backup-and-restore.md
# for the command and for the cron line that runs it nightly.

# UF_BACKUP_DIR=./backups

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the container may take from the host.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The defaults suit the shipped concurrency settings — 4 runs at a time plus 2
# other Claude processes. Raise them together with those settings, not after:
# roughly 1.5 GiB per concurrent run, 0.5 GiB per other Claude process and
# 2.5 GiB for the server. Every agent is a full Claude Code process that then
# starts builds and test suites of its own inside the same container, so memory
# is linear in how many runs you let go at once. Raising a setting alone gets
# the container OOM-killed under load; raising a limit alone does nothing. See
# README's "Sizing the container" for the arithmetic and a worked example at 25
# runs.
#
# Set the limit below the RAM you are willing to hand this container — one
# above what the machine can supply is not a limit, and the host's OOM killer
# is back in scope with the whole machine as its blast radius. compose pins
# memswap_limit to the same figure, which is how Docker spells "no swap".

# UF_MEM_LIMIT=10g

# The server's own heap ceiling, in MiB. Stated rather than left to V8, which
# derives it from the *host's* RAM — so the one term of the arithmetic above
# that belongs to this process would otherwise change with the machine. It does
# not scale with the fleet, and every child environment strips it, so it bounds
# the server and never the `claude` children it spawns.
# UF_NODE_HEAP_MB=2048

# Tasks, not processes, and generous on purpose: hitting it fails a fork inside
# an agent's tool call, where the runaway it exists to bound would otherwise
# exhaust the host's pid space.
# UF_PIDS_LIMIT=2048

# CPUs this container may use, in cores. Blank is the shipped state and it is
# no ceiling at all rather than a generous one: compose renders `cpus:
# ${UF_CPUS:-0}` and 0 is Docker's own word for "no limit", so the container may
# take every core the machine has while several agents compile at once. No
# figure can be shipped in its place, because Docker refuses a `cpus` larger
# than the host's CPU count — a number sized for a 12-core machine would make
# `docker compose up` fail outright on a 4-core one, which is a wrong answer to
# a correct configuration.
#
# Which of the two a running container is in, from the cgroup rather than from
# this file:
#
#   docker compose exec usagefoundry cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max
#   # `max 100000` is no quota; `800000 100000` is the 8 cores of UF_CPUS=8
#
# Set it to leave the machine a core or two — `nproc` minus one is the usual
# choice. What that buys is the host staying responsive under a busy fleet, so
# you can still reach this app and stop it; it does not protect the server's own
# event loop from the children it supervises, which share the same quota.
# UF_CPUS=
